<!--Post: 5632--><!--Time: 1107226625-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5632"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Researcher<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Researcher<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, January 31, 2005 - 6:57 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->After viewing the postcard that BTK sent to KAKE last week I am bothered - again - by his writing as compared to his use of the Eglish language re: the Nancy Fox telephone call. <BR> <BR>The address on the postcard - specifically the from and to section are inconsistent. <BR> <BR>In the from section he uses lower case spelling Wichita and omits the period after KS.  In the address section he uses all caps and includes the period after KA. <BR> <BR>The S Killett return address is lined up, but the KAKE address section seems to fall to the right as if typed with a typewriter. I am curious as to whether he used a typewriter, or a computer using typewriter fonts. <BR> <BR>The directions portion is also interesting. The use of the colons are again not consistent. He uses a space before inserting it after the word Contents. In the other sections he does not. He also mispelled Toasties as Tosties.  <BR> <BR>BTK&#39;s use of the colons reminds me of the format sometimes used by the military, and law enforcement agencies when writing a memo.  I have used that format myself many times when writing internal memos/e-mails. <BR> <BR>BTK used the words &#34;some how&#34; for somehow, and omitted the article &#39;the&#39; before Wichita PD. And when inquiring about drop #7 he again omits the article &#39;the&#39; before PD. <BR> <BR>Again it is the Nancy Fox audio tape of his voice that bothers me.  He sounds artiuculate &#40;as far as we can tell from the short conversation&#41; and his use of the phrase &#34;that is correct&#34; instead of &#34;that&#39;s right&#34; seems to indictate someone who is well spoken. <BR> <BR>Although people often do not write like they talk, BTK&#39;s omissions, misuse of words, and spelling errors seem to be consistent with what has been revealed from his communications over the years.  Some theorize that was done on purpose.  I am not so sure that is the case at all. <BR> <BR>Lastly his use of the date is again not consistent - 1/17/2005 versus 1/8/05 &#40;not 2005&#41;. <BR> <BR>Guess I think of BTK as careful and meticilous. For whatever reason, his writing skills show just the opposite. <BR> <BR>Can&#39;t figure it out.  What does it mean?  Maybe nothing.  Just passing it on because it bugs me -as perhaps it does to most of you. <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5632-->
<!--Post: 6472--><!--Time: 1107809555-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6472"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Witchking<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Witchking<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, February 07, 2005 - 12:52 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I agree with you completely on the voice pattern. See my other posting on my thoughts on this clue<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6472-->
<!--Post: 6488--><!--Time: 1107823282-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6488"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Iceteajan<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Iceteajan<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, February 07, 2005 - 4:41 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Researcher, <BR> <BR>You pose an interesting question:  Why is he supposedly so meticulous, yet delivers sloppy communications? <BR> <BR>Perhaps his sloppy postcard is really due to poor typing skills.   <BR> <BR>Or perhaps, he has only a short span of time to &#34;hijack&#34; the typewriter and is too harried to think about mistakes. <BR> <BR>Provided he is using an &#34;old&#34; typewriter, then lining things up can be pain. <BR> <BR>I tend to think he is NOT using a modern word processing application &#40;where he could easily review and correct his errors before sending to print&#41;. <BR> <BR>Also, feeding a postcard through the newer printers these days is not impossible, but is a bit tricky. <BR> <BR>I tend to think he is using an old typewriter. <BR> <BR>Also we assume he is meticulous -- well perhaps not -- perhaps he just has &#34;dumb luck&#34; coupled with a ton of nerve. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6488-->
